// A **standalone** `impl Trait for Type` is part of its type wherever the type goes — including
// into a consumer that never names the type. The implementor here (`pkg.lib`'s `Hidden`) is not
// `pub` and is never imported; it reaches this program only because another standalone impl's body
// returns one, and its `impl Decoder for Hidden` has to travel with it.
//
// It did not. The linker merged a pooled standalone impl only when its target type was already in
// the merged program, decided from a snapshot taken *before* any impl body's own dependency closure
// ran — so the impl that pulls the implementor in always ran too late to make the implementor's own
// impl eligible, and no source order could fix that (`Late` below is written the other way round for
// exactly that reason). The type still linked and its inherent methods still dispatched; only its
// trait membership went missing, so the library's own tests stayed green and every *consumer* broke
// with "expected `dyn Decoder`, found `Hidden`". Writing the same impl in the class body worked, so
// two spellings of one declaration disagreed across a module boundary.
//
// Every surface that reads the membership table is pinned, because they all read the same one: the
// `dyn Decoder` coercion, trait-method dispatch, the precise `is dyn Trait` test, a `<T: Decoder>`
// bound, and `traits_of`. A **directory** case on purpose — a single-file case never goes through
// the loader, so it cannot observe any of this.
// expect: stdout "HI"
// expect: stdout "DIRECT"
// expect: stdout "BOUND"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "[\"pkg.lib.Decoder\"]"
// expect: stdout "late-hi"
// expect: exit 0
namespace pkg.main;

use pkg.lib.{MyCodec, Codec};

MyCodec.new().run()
